 { In such endings with a bishop against pawns on the same flank, a main weapon for the stronger side is the gradual use of zugzwang. Of course he should also be on his guard against pawn exchanges. } 1... { -KEY- } Ke4 $1 { King first! } ( 1... Bf6 $6 2. g5 { would be less technical. } ) 
2. f3+ ( { Or } 2. g5 Kf4 3. g6 Kg4 { and the pawns fall by themselves. } ) 
Ke3 3. g5 { Lacking better options, White has to expose his pawns. } Kf4 4. Kf2 ( { Again, } 4. g6 Kf5 5. h5 { makes no sense for White in view of } Bg7 $1 { , but not } Kg5 $4 6. f4 $1 { on the topic of deflection. } ) 
Bh8 $1 { The bishop is an excellent executer of the zugzwang, as we already saw in Example 54 (Hellsten-Fernandez), among others. } 5. Ke2 ( { Or } 5. Kg2 Bd4 $1 6. g6 Kf5 7. Kh3 Kxg6 8. Kg4 Bf6 { and Black wins comfortably; e.g. } 9. h5 Kh6 10. f4 Be7 11. f5 e5 ) 
Kg3 ( { There could have followed } 5... Kg3 6. Ke3 Kxh4 7. f4 Kg4 8. Ke4 Bg7 $1 { (zugzwang) } 9. Ke3 Kf5 10. Kf3 Bf8 { followed by ...Bd6xf4. } ) 

