 { Despite Black's active king, White has a serious advantage here thanks to her superior bishop. Pytel now starts some subtle manoeuvring with this piece, in which zugzwang plays a key role. We saw a similar endgame in Example 22 (Averbakh-Matanovic) in Chapter One. } 1. { -KEY- } Be2 Bd7 ( { On } 1... h5 { there would follow } 2. Bd3 Bf7 3. Bh7 $1 { (losing a tempo) } Be8 4. Bc2 Bf7 5. a4 $1 { with similar play as in the game. } ) 
2. Bd1 $1 { An initial zugzwang - Black has to allow a3-a4. } Be6 ( 2... h5 { is much the same thing as the previous note. } ) 
3. a4 $1 { A new weakness is established on b5. } Bd7 ( { After } 3... bxa4 4. Bxa4 { the a6-pawn becomes a decisive weakness; e.g. } Bf5 5. Bd1 Bd7 6. Be2 Bc8 7. h5 $1 { (zugzwang) } Kf5 8. Kd4 Bb7 9. Bf1 { (again) } Kg5 10. Ke5 { and wins - Muller, since } Kxh5 11. Kf6 -- { and } 12. Bd3 { is terminal. } ) 
4. axb5 axb5 5. Be2 $1 { Again zugzwang, this time with the purpose of provoking ...h6-h5. } h5 6. Bf1 Bc6 7. Bd3 Be8 8. Bc2 Bf7 9. Bh7 $1 { Losing a tempo, one of the bishop's specialties in the endgame. } Be8 10. Bd3 { Another zugzwang. With the bishop tied to both b5 and g6, Black finally has to move her king. } Ke6 ( { Or } 10... d4 11. cxd4 Kd5 12. Bf1 Bd7 13. Kd3 Bf5 14. Kc3 Bd7 15. Bg2 Ke6 16. Be4 { and wins - Muller. } ) 
11. Kd4 Kd6 12. Bf5 Bf7 13. Bh7 $1 { The idea is repeated. } Be8 ( { If } 13... Ke6 { then } 14. Kc5 Ke5 15. Bd3 $1 { - Muller. } ) 
14. Bg8 $1 ( { a) In view of } 14. Bg8 Bc6 15. Bf7 { Black resigned. } ) 
 ( { b) By the way, } 14. Bd3 { with a fourth zugzwang was possible too. } ) 

 1-0

