 { As seen in Barhudarian-Rozentalis, among others, one virtue of placing one's rook behind the passed pawn is the ability to make waiting moves with it, while the opponent can't do the same thing. Here is another case. } 1. { -KEY- } Kc4 $1 { White gets ready to assist the passed pawn by 2.Kb5, but also enables a second plan: bring the king to e3 and then wait for Black to run out of moves. } ( { Instead, the game went } 1. g4 $2 Kg5 { (now the black king has a clear target - Baburin) } 2. Kc4 e4 3. Kb5 Re6 4. a6 e3 5. a7 Re5 6. Kc6 Re8 7. a8=Q $11 Rxa8 8. Rxa8 Kxg4 { and was soon drawn. } ) 
 ( 1. Kc3 { is less forcing, e.g. } h5 { (such space-gaining moves are rarely in vain) } 2. Kd3 Rd6 3. Ke3 Ra6 { followed by waiting moves with the king. Contrary to the main line, here it's more difficult for White to get at the e-pawn, which explains why the reply } e4 { is good news for them. } ) 
e4 ( 1... h5 { now invites } 2. Kb5 { , when after } Ra8 3. a6 e4 4. Kc4 $1 Kg4 5. Kd4 Kf3 6. Ra3 { , White just makes it to eliminate the e-pawn without losing track of the a-pawn. } ) 
2. Kd4 $1 ( { After } 2. Kb5 $2 Ra8 3. a6 e3 { Black again has counterplay. } ) 
g5 ( { Or } 2... Rd6 3. Ke3 Ra6 4. h3 { in similar fashion. } ) 
3. Ke3 g4 ( { In the event of } 3... h5 { one possible reply is } 4. h3 { avoiding pawn exchanges. } ) 
4. Ra1 $1 { From now on, White just makes waiting moves with the rook until zugzwang is reached. } Ke5 5. Ra2 Kd5 6. Ra4 h6 { Evidently, any king move would lose the e4-pawn, while rook moves just make things worse by allowing a5-a6. } 7. Ra1 Ke5 8. Ra2 Kd5 9. Ra4 { Repeating the process. } h5 10. Ra1 Ke5 11. Ra2 Kd5 12. Ra4 { A final zugzwang, forcing Black to abandon the e4-pawn. White wins - Baburin. } 
