 { On occasion, the king can lose a tempo by moving in a triangle, supposing that the enemy king can't do likewise. We saw a related case already inMoreno-Suba (in Chapter One) ; here are some more examples of such a triangulation.A quick glance reveals that White would very much like to reach this position with the opponent to move, so that he can play Kd6 without bothering about ...Kb6. } 1. { -KEY- } Kc4 $1 { Starting a triangulation. } ( { a) In contrast, the game went } 1. Kd6 $6 Kb6 2. Ke5 Kxb5 3. Kf6 Ka4 4. Kxg6 b5 5. f5 b4 6. f6 b3 7. f7 b2 8. f8=Q $11 b1=Q { with decent drawing chances for Black in the queen ending, although he lost it many moves later. } ) 
 ( { b) Not all triangles are the same; e.g. } 1. Kb4 $4 Kb6 2. Kc4 Ka5 3. Kc5 { and White has admittedly achieved one, but after } Ka4 $1 4. Kb6 Kb4 5. Kxb7 Kxb5 { it is Black who arrives first at the enemy pawns. } ) 
Kb6 ( { Or } 1... Kb8 2. Kd5 Kc7 3. Ke5 Kb6 4. Kf6 Kxb5 5. Kxg6 { and the f-pawn decides. } ) 
2. Kb4 $1 Kc7 ( { White's task is even easier after } 2... Ka7 3. Kc5 Kb8 4. Kd6 Kc8 5. Ke6 { etc - Eszik. } ) 
3. Kc5 b6+ ( 3... Kd7 4. Kd5 Ke7 5. Ke5 b6 6. f5 $1 { transposes to the main line. } ) 
4. Kd5 Kd7 5. Ke5 Ke7 6. f5 $1 { This breakthrough wins a decisive pawn. } gxf5 7. Kxf5 Kf7 ( { Or } 7... Kd6 8. Kg5 Kc5 9. Kxh5 Kxb5 10. Kg5 { and only White queens. } ) 
8. Kg5 Kg7 9. Kxh5 Kh7 10. Kg5 Kg7 11. Kf5 { With a quick win. } 
 *

