 { Triangulation can also occur in other types of endings, as in the next few examples.In order to win White should hand over the turn to the opponent. } 1. { -KEY- } Kb6 $1 { Initiating some kind of triangulation. } ( { a) Evidently, } 1. c5 $2 { was premature due to } Kd4 ) 
 ( { b) And } 1. Kd7 Kf6 2. Ke8 $6 Kg7 { isn't productive either. } ) 
 ( { c) But another triangle with } 1. Kb5 $5 Kd4 ( { or } 1. Kb6 Be7 2. c5 $1 Kxd5 3. c6 Kd6 4. Kb6 { etc. } ) 
2. Kb6 $1 Ke5 3. Kc6 { also works } ) 
 ( { d) As does } 1. Kd7 Kf6 2. Kc7 $1 Ke5 3. Kc6 ) 
Kd4 { The most tenacious reply. } ( { Both } 1... Kd6 { and } Be7 { run into the sacrifice } 2. c5 $1 Kxd5 3. c6 { and one of the pawns queens. } ) 
2. Kc7 Ke5 3. Kc6 { The triangulation is completed, and Black now lacks a solid move. } Kf6 ( { a) If } 3... Kd4 { then } 4. Kd7 Ke5 5. Ke8 ) 
 ( { b) And } 3... Be7 4. Kd7 $1 Kf6 5. Ke8 ) 
 ( { c) Or } 3... Bd6 4. Kd7 { is similar. } ) 
 ( { d) Finally, } 3... Ba3 { fails to } 4. c5 ) 
4. c5 Ke5 5. Kb6 $1 { Clearing the way for the c-pawn, without bothering about the bishop. } ( 5. Kb5 Kxd5 6. c6 Kd6 7. Kb6 { is just as good. } ) 
Kxd5 6. c6 { White wins. } 
