 { Next, let's examine the subject of waiting moves with the pawns. In pawn endings the availability of such moves can often determine the final result; thus one should use them with care. Here are some examples.White wins by the opportune use of waiting moves with the pawns. } 1. { -KEY- } Kh4 $1 { King first! } Ke6 2. Kg5 Kf7 3. f4 { Given that White still has two spare tempi left with the h-pawn, he can afford to use up those with the f-pawn, in order to prevent any counterplay with ...f5-f4. } ( { a) Nevertheless, the move orders } 3. f3 Kg7 4. f4 Kf7 5. Kh6 Kf6 6. h4 $1 ) 
 ( { b) And } 3. Kh6 $5 Kf6 ( { b1) or } 3. f4 f4 4. gxf4 Kf6 5. h4 $1 Kf5 6. f3 Kf6 7. Kh7 Kf7 8. f5 $1 gxf5 9. f4 ) 
4. h4 ( { b2) or } 4. f4 Kf7 5. h3 ) 
 ( { b3) but not } 4. Kh7 $6 f4 $1 ) 
Kf7 5. Kh7 Kf6 6. f3 $1 ( { b4) not } 6. Kg8 $2 f4 $1 7. gxf4 Kf5 8. Kf7 Kxf4 9. Kxg6 Kg4 ) 
Kf7 7. f4 { work as well. } ) 
Kg7 4. h3 $1 ( { Seeing that } 4. h4 $2 Kf7 5. Kh6 Kf6 { just draws, White keeps a last waiting move in reserve. } ) 
Kh7 ( { Or } 4... Kf7 5. Kh6 Kf6 6. h4 Kf7 7. Kh7 { with similar effect. } ) 
5. Kf6 Kh6 ( { If } 5... h4 { then } 6. gxh4 Kh6 7. Kf7 Kh7 8. h5 { etc. } ) 
6. h4 $1 { White finally uses up his last waiting move in order to gain the opposition. } ( 6. Kf7 { is another winning possibility, but reducing Black's options for counterplay such as ...g6-g5 and ...h5-h4 makes sense from a practical perspective. } ) 
Kh7 7. Kf7 Kh6 8. Kg8 { White wins. } 
 *

