 { Here is a final pawn ending, in which the waiting moves with the pawns played a crucial role, not only in the actual course of the game, but also in several of its subvariations. Thanks to her more active king Black is a bit better. In order to keep the king on d3, White now desires to make a waiting move with a pawn, but which one? } 1. { -KEY- } h4 $2 { A bad choice, since after the opponent's reply, White will be short of spare tempi on the kingside. } ( { For this reason, } 1. g4 $1 { was much better; e.g. } g5 2. fxg5 hxg5 3. Ke3 b5 4. Kd3 { and if now } bxa4 5. bxa4 e5 $2 { then White's spare tempo even leads her to a full point: } ( 5. h4 Kd6 { is correct, with a draw } ) 
6. h3 $1 Kb6 7. Kc4 Kb7 8. Kc5 Kc7 9. c4 $1 { and wins. } ) 
h5 $1 2. Ke3 { Now a waiting move with the king is the most solid choice. } ( { a) If instead } 2. f5 $2 { then } exf5 3. exf5 Kd5 { and the f5-pawn falls } ) 
 ( { b) While } 2. c4 $2 { fails to } Kb4 3. Kc2 Ka3 4. Kc3 c5 $1 5. Kc2 Ka2 6. f5 e5 7. Kc3 Kb1 { and Black wins, a variation similar toExample 352 (Nabaty-Arnaudov) with reversed colours and flanks. } ) 
b5 $1 { This advance either creates an outside passed pawn, or softens up the c4-square for the king. } 3. Kd3 ( { After } 3. axb5 cxb5 4. Kd3 a4 5. b4 Kc6 6. Kc2 f5 $1 { the king enters via d5 with great effect. } ) 
bxa4 4. bxa4 { Now Black just needs to lose a tempo in order to make the white king step back, but how to do it? } f5 $1 { The correct reaction. } ( 4... g6 $2 { has a surprising flaw. } 5. c4 $2 { An early surrender. } ( { The unexpected breakthrough } 4... f5 4. g4 $1 { led to a draw: } hxg4 { (after } f5 $2 6. g5 $1 { White even wins; e.g. } Kd6 7. exf5 exf5 8. Kc4 Kd7 9. Kc5 Kc7 10. c4 { , etc.) } 6. f5 exf5 7. exf5 Kd6 $1 8. fxg6 Ke7 { , etc, on the topic of mutual protected passed pawns. } ) 
Kb4 6. Kd4 { (It is already too late for } 6. g4 { because of } f5 $1 { ) } c5 { and the rest is easy: } 7. Kd3 Kxa4 8. Kc3 Ka3 9. e5 f5 10. Kd3 Kb4 { . } ) 
5. e5 g6 ( 5... Kd5 { also works, e.g. } 6. c4 Kc5 7. Kc3 g6 8. Kd3 Kb4 9. Kd4 c5 $1 { etc. } ) 
6. Kd2 Kc4 7. Kc2 c5 ( { And Black wins. Incidentally, we came across a similar variation in the } 0... c5 0. g4 { subline, with reversed colors. } ) 

 0-1

