 { Next, let's see two examples where the stronger side decided to transpose into a pawn ending, anticipating a later usage of waiting moves with pawns.Black has just played ...Kg8-g7, but he should have placed his king on h7 instead. The difference becomes evident with White's reply. } 1. { -KEY- } Qf6+ $1 { Korchnoi has observed that in the pawn ending that now arises, despite the material balance he will be winning thanks to his spare tempi with the f-pawn. } Qxf6 2. exf6+ Kxf6 3. Kf4 $1 { Opposition, and a first zugzwang. } Ke6 ( { After } 3... Kg7 4. Ke5 Kg8 ( { or } 4... f6 5. Ke6 g5 6. f4 $1 { with zugzwang to come } ) 
5. Kf6 Kf8 6. f4 $1 { followed by f4-f5, White soon wins a decisive pawn - Furhoff. } ) 
4. Kg5 Ke5 ( { Or } 4... Ke7 5. f3 $1 { with similar effect. } ) 
5. f3 $1 { A second zugzwang. } ( { In contrast, } 5. f4 $2 Ke6 { is just a draw; e.g. } ( { or } 5. f3 Ke4 ) 
6. f5 gxf5 7. Kxh5 f4 $1 { However, with Black to move again after 5...Ke6 the outcome is different, as we will see next. } ) 
Ke6 6. f4 ( { In view of } 6. f4 Ke7 7. f5 $1 gxf5 8. Kxf5 f6 9. Kg6 { Black resigned. } ) 

 1-0

