 { White found a clear-cut solution to the realization of his extra pawn. } 1. { -KEY- } Bxc6 $1 Kxc6 2. b4 $1 ( { A key advance that secures a spare tempo with a2-a3, and leaves Black with none. A good alternative is } 2. Kb4 $5 { in 'King first' spirit, as observed by hobbsey, who explains that after } Kd5 3. Ka5 { White is much faster in the upcoming passed pawns race, while if } Kb6 { trying to keep White's king at bay, then after } 3. a4 $1 a5 4. Kb3 { the white king soon enters anyway, e.g. } b4 5. Kc4 Kc6 6. b3 { . } ) 
 ( { Interestingly, even } 2. a4 { first would make the trick, e.g. } bxa4 3. Kc4 { followed by d4-d5, when the absence of a black pawn on b5 means that White's king can alternate between d4 and c4 in order to make Black run out of moves. } ) 
Kd6 3. Kd2 $1 { Heading for e4, while applying some kind of distant opposition. } ( { The straightforward } 3. Kd3 Kd5 4. Ke3 { also works, since } ( { of course not } 4. a3 $2 { wasting the spare move, with a draw after } Kc6 5. Ke4 Kd6 6. d5 Kd7 { etc } ) 
Kc4 { can be met by } 5. Ke4 Kxb4 6. Kd5 $1 Ka3 7. Kc5 { with 8.d5 next. } ) 
Kc6 ( { Or } 3... Kd5 4. Kd3 { transposing. } ) 
4. Ke3 Kd5 ( { If } 4... Kd7 { then } 5. Ke4 Kd6 6. d5 Kd7 7. Kd4 Kd6 8. a3 $1 { etc. } ) 
5. Kd3 { Here Black resigned. Let's see a possible continuation: } Kd6 6. Ke4 Kc6 7. d5+ Kd7 8. Kd4 Kd6 9. a3 $1 { The spare tempo comes in very handy at this point. } Kd7 10. Kc5 Kc7 11. d6+ Kd7 12. Kd5 { White wins. } 
