 { In this final example, the transposition to a pawn ending favours the defender instead, but for the same reason as before: a future usage of waiting moves with the pawns.Despite the doubled f-pawns White is better here, thanks to his more active rook and king, along with the weakness on a5. } 1. { -KEY- } Rxe6+ $2 { A tempting, but wrong choice. } ( { White should have preferred either } 1. Rb7 Kf6 2. Rxh7 Rd6 3. Ke4 Rb6 4. Rd7 ) 
 ( { or } 1. Rb5 $5 { keeping some winning chances. } ) 
Kxe6 2. Kc4 { Evidently, the a5-pawn is doomed, but Black has one last trump available. } g5 $1 { Fixing the kingside structure so as to secure two extra tempi with the h-pawn later on. } ( { In contrast, after } 2... Kd6 3. Kb5 Kd5 $2 ( { it is not yet too late for } 3... g5 $1 ) 
4. Kxa5 Kc5 5. h4 $1 { Black's waiting moves soon end, which lets the white king return to the battle: } h6 6. Ka6 Kc6 7. a5 h5 ( { or } 7... g5 8. hxg5 hxg5 9. Ka7 Kc7 10. a6 ) 
8. Ka7 Kc7 9. a6 Kc8 10. Kb6 { and White wins. } ) 
3. Kb5 Kd5 4. Kxa5 Kc5 5. h3 ( { Or } 5. Ka6 Kc6 6. a5 h6 $1 7. h3 h5 { transposing. } ) 
h6 $1 { Economizing with the spare tempi. } 6. Ka6 Kc6 7. a5 h5 8. Ka7 Kc7 9. a6 h4 { It transpires that White can't liberate his king. } 10. Ka8 Kc8 11. Ka7 
